# **The Business Model of Khalid Farhan in Bangladesh**

![](THE%20POLITICS%20OF%20MUTUAL%20BENEFIT%20\(4).png)\
If you are a fan of Khalid Farhan and his contents, then you are part of the reasons which made him a millionaire. His marketing skills justify his net worth, I don’t argue with it, I have strong support for it.

There is a famous term in marketing: making customers the heroes. This explains a lot about the business model of Khalid in Bangladesh. I learnt this phrase from a well-established book, *Building a StoryBrand* by Donald Miller. Interestingly, it was suggested by Khalid Farhan himself.

This brilliant guy is mostly popular in Bangladesh for making Bangla videos with eye-catching headlines like “Creating another Uber in 5 min using Claude”, “e-commerce website in 10 min”, “how to be rich” and shit.

Realistically? It is never possible to make such a practical website within 5 min. I don’t think it is even possible to make a good prompt within 5 minutes. At least it takes 15+ min to write a good-quality prompt with an average 100 WPM typing speed. For a voice draft, minimum 5. Okay, well, I am a noob against Khalid Farhan, and let’s assume he is one of the best in prompting, can make the best possible prompt before you can even count to a minute. But what about the time for Claude to create it? You think that is possible? I suggest going and practically trying it yourself. See how long it takes to make even some simple websites and content using Claude. I bet Khalid knows this really well, but why does he still gamble with his words?

Well, this question brings us to his targeted market.

Most people, especially regular audiences and customers in Bangladesh, are not necessarily interested in the actual process. They are attracted to the possibility. Most people simply highlight continuously that he is a scammer, too much of a commercial marketer, and doesn’t actually want to help people beyond his business — all of these criticisms can have some truth to them, but they miss the exact business model he applies in the Bangladesh market, which he has indirectly, or maybe mistakenly, revealed himself.

So basically, people in Bangladesh love to dream way more than doing, and making things real.

I believe most of us have heard that when we dream of something too much or talk about something with others, we already get a dopamine hit from the imagined achievement and become less interested in doing the actual work. And Khalid is an expert at making people feel like they are the hero. They can easily make anything. They can become rich. They can build an Uber.

The audience loved his words and felt something inside. They think anyone can build an Uber now, anyone can make money too easily and shit.

But nobody does. Why?

They fail before even starting.

This has also been acknowledged very well by Khalid himself. And that’s why I think he takes the risk. That’s why you rarely hear the things I just wrote from him. He understands that people don’t necessarily buy the hard process. They buy the feeling that the hard process is now easy.

People nowadays don’t even bother to think strategically or logically with real-world arguments. Some guy literally started thinking that critical thinking and arguments are some sort of lazy and repetitive work in 2026, and that we need to “catch up with the speed of AI.”

Well, I am not the right one to judge a human. I just saw nobody talking about this, and that’s why I brought the argument up here.

I don’t claim myself to be perfectly right. I’m open to discussion on it, and always ready to take back any argument if I get something more logical.
